From 27d076146f86ede5db18973e4ce053bf036c6976 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Julien Etienne <julien.etienne@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 06:29:52 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: mt6797: send ordinary data at mac80211's rate control
 choice

mac80211 already runs minstrel_ht for this hardware: the driver does not
claim its own rate control. But every ordinary data frame was sent at
the fixed descriptor rate, and the status counts were cleared, so the
rate control's choice was discarded and it never learnt anything.

Wire it up behind CONFIG_MT6797_DATA_RATE_CONTROL, or rate=minstrel in a
measurement build's per-join schedule. The latter was refused until now
because the wiring was missing.
- An ordinary data frame's descriptor takes info->control.rates[0]:
  - an HT MCS of one stream as HT mixed mode, 0x80 | MCS;
  - IEEE80211_TX_RC_40_MHZ_WIDTH as FIX_BW_40;
  - IEEE80211_TX_RC_SHORT_GI as u2FixedRate's guard-interval bit;
  - a legacy rate as its bitrate's hw_value, which now carries the
    vendor rate code (CCK long preamble and OFDM 6-54 Mbit/s) instead of
    the table index.
- A reported frame's status names that rate with the MCU's attempt
  count.
- A frame sent at the fixed rate, or at a rate the descriptor cannot
  carry, reports rate index -1, so the rate control learns nothing
  false from it.
- hw->max_rates is 1 and max_rate_tries matches the descriptor's three
  attempts.

With credit retire, only the 1-in-N sampled frames carry a status
report. So every rate-control probe now requests one as well; otherwise
no probe's result would ever be learnt. Management, security and EAPOL
frames keep their fixed rates.

The rate sent is reported through storage the two fields share:
info->control.rates and info->status.rates are the same bytes of one
union, and ieee80211_tx_info_clear_status keeps the rates. So a reported
frame's status already names the rate it was sent at, and only the
count is set. A BUILD_BUG_ON fails the build if that layout ever
changes. For the same reason, a frame that falls back to the fixed rate
reports no rate by setting that index to -1.

The data path should never take the fallback while rate control is
enabled. If it does, the frame is counted in rate_fallbacks on the test
stats line, reset per lifetime. A 40 MHz rate is used only on the HT40
join (secondary offset 3). On a 20 MHz association a stray width flag
takes the fallback instead of putting wide frames, or a wrong status
rate, on the link.

Tested on a W=1 arm64 build of the driver with
CONFIG_MT6797_DATA_RATE_CONTROL off and on. Host tests:
run-transfer-size-test.py against its pre-0310 parent,
run-testcfg-test.py (including the tx-rate mapping test),
run-ht40-hif-test.py (seven builds) and run-hif-rx-batch-test.py.
